          "text": "<S0>Let's think step by step:\n\n1. The query asks: \"How much does return shipping cost if I just do not like my boots?\"  \n   This is asking about return shipping cost for a non-defective, personal preference-based return (boots).\n\n2. The context states:\n   - Return shipping is free for defective items.\n   - For all other returns, the customer pays 6 EUR.\n   - Tents that have been pitched are considered used and cannot be returned.\n   - Sale items can only be exchanged, never refunded.\n   - Gift cards are not refundable.\n\n3. The boots are not defective and are not tents, so they fall under \"all other returns\" — which means the customer pays 6 EUR.\n\n4. The response correctly identifies:\n   - Return shipping is 6 EUR for non-defective items.\n   - The boots are not defective.\n   - Therefore, the cost is 6 EUR.\n\n5. The response does not introduce any incorrect or unrelated information.\n6. It directly answers the query using only information from the context.\n7. It correctly applies the rule for non-defective returns.\n\nThus, the response is fully grounded in the context, accurate, and directly addresses the query.\n\nTherefore, it meets the definition of Groundedness: 5.</S0>\n<S1>The response accurately and completely answers the query using only information from the context, without introducing errors or irrelevant details.</S1>\n<S2>5</S2>"
